<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);TUnitAssertions0005</NoWarn>
    <!-- Shared fixture library: referenced by test projects, never shipped. -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      #1809: TWO references were dead weight and are gone.

        * Harbor.Application — NOT a single type from it is used. Every type
          TestKit touches was attributed to its declaring assembly: 22 types
          from Harbor.Abstractions (IAgent, ITool, ILlmClient, ToolContext,
          ToolDescriptor, SystemPromptContext, ...), 26 from
          Harbor.Abstractions.Contracts (Session, AgentMessage, Usage, Pricing,
          ToolName, PermissionRuleset, ProviderId, ...), 2 from
          Harbor.Desktop.ViewModels (ChatViewModelBase, DiffViewModelBase),
          2 from Harbor.Ui.Framework.Services, 3 from
          Harbor.Ui.Framework.State, 1 from Harbor.Ui.Framework.ViewModels.
          The only mention of Harbor.Application anywhere in the project was
          a COMMENT (AllocationProbe.cs names Harbor.Application.Tests).
        * Harbor.Diagnostics.Abstractions — 0 usages as well; it declares 8
          types (ITracer/IMetrics/correlation) and TestKit names none of them.

      Every remaining reference is load-bearing and is declared explicitly,
      not leaned on transitively. Note that the preset catalogue is STILL in
      TestKit's transitive closure — via Harbor.Desktop.ViewModels, which
      needs ProviderPresets for the onboarding view-models (the documented #188
      Presentation->Application exception). Removing the direct edge therefore
      does NOT shrink the closure; do not re-add it "for symmetry" — the
      honest fix for the remaining path is a separate move of ProviderPresets,
      tracked outside #1809.

      #1899 renamed that one reference: the two view-model bases moved out of
      Harbor.Desktop.Abstractions (which is now the config-schema leaf) into
      Harbor.Desktop.ViewModels, and the #188 edge moved with them, so the
      assembly that drags the catalogue in is the one the bases live in now —
      and since #1889 that assembly is reached from the sibling
      `Harbor.TestKit.Tui`, not from this core. Since #1625 the catalogue
      itself lives in Harbor.PolicyConfig, so that edge is
      Harbor.Desktop.ViewModels -> Harbor.PolicyConfig; the namespace it is
      declared in is still Harbor.Application.Configuration.
    -->
    <!--
      #1889: the four UI references are GONE, and with them the closure
      shrinks from 19 assemblies to 4.

      The files that needed them — `UiTestDoubles.cs` (the dispatcher double
      and the counting view-model stubs), `NullRenderContext.cs` and
      `TestPanelProvider.cs` — now live in the sibling project
      `Harbor.TestKit.Tui`, which is the ONE place allowed to pull the
      UI/Desktop stack in on behalf of a test. What stayed here is the core:
      agent fakes, scripted clients, assertions, message builders, perf gates
      — none of which touch a view model.

      The paragraph above is kept as a record, not as a description of this
      file: the "Harbor.Application is STILL in TestKit's transitive closure"
      sentence was true until #1889 and is now false, because the edge that
      carried it (Harbor.Desktop.ViewModels since #1899 split the desktop
      view-models out) left with the doubles. The
      ProviderPresets move it mentions is still worth doing — it is what keeps
      `Harbor.Desktop.ViewModels` heavy for every project that needs a
      desktop view-model base — but it is no longer what makes TestKit heavy.

      The closure is FOUR, not three, and the fourth is the kit's OWN support
      part: `Harbor.TestKit.Support` (see the #1772/#1773 note below). It is
      not a product assembly — it references `Harbor.Abstractions` only — so
      the core still adds no product assembly to any consumer beyond
      `Harbor.Abstractions` and `Harbor.Abstractions.Contracts`.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/Harbor.Abstractions/Harbor.Abstractions.csproj"/>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/Harbor.Abstractions.Contracts/Harbor.Abstractions.Contracts.csproj"/>
    <!--
      #1772/#1773: the helpers that need no product assembly beyond the
      contracts (the three scopes, the logging doubles, the Abstractions-level
      doubles) live in the sibling `Harbor.TestKit.Support`, so that test
      projects which do NOT have the Ui.Framework / Terminal / Application
      family in their closure can still reach ONE implementation of them.
      Referencing the sibling here means a consumer of this project gets those
      types transitively, in the same `Harbor.TestKit` namespace, without
      adding a product assembly to anybody's closure.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../Harbor.TestKit.Support/Harbor.TestKit.Support.csproj"/>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    #1569: the RESULT ASSERTIONS left this project too. `ResultAssertions.cs`
    is now in the sibling `Harbor.TestKit.Support`, next to the shared doubles
    that need no product assembly either.

    The reason is the same one that put the doubles there: the assertions need
    the two CONTRACT assemblies and two PACKAGES, and nothing else — so the
    24 test projects that take only the support part can now reach them
    without pulling the core kit (the agent fakes, the scripted clients, the
    two cost gates) into their closure.

    Nothing about a consumer's source changes. The namespace is still
    `Harbor.TestKit` (the support part sets `<RootNamespace>Harbor.TestKit`),
    and this project still references the support part, so the thirteen
    projects that take the core keep the assertions transitively.
  -->

  <!--
    #1875: the source-generated test doubles LEFT this project. The
    generator (`tests/Harbor.TestKit.Generators`) now runs over the sibling
    `Harbor.TestKit.Generated`, and so does the opt-in marker
    (`GenerateFakesAttribute.cs`) and the call-record type
    (`Generated/FakeCall.cs`). This project therefore no longer declares the
    generator as an analyzer at all — the whole ItemGroup is gone, not just
    the reference.

    The two halves of that move:

      * the CORE gets smaller. The 12 generated classes and their 48 methods
        are no longer compiled into the assembly that 19 test projects
        reference, so the core's own surface — and its binary — shrink.
      * the DOUBLES get their own closure of three: themselves and the two
        contract assemblies. A test project that needs a double and nothing
        else takes that part, not this one.

    The namespace did not change: the generator hard-codes
    `Harbor.TestKit.Generated` and the marker is matched by its metadata name
    `Harbor.TestKit.GenerateFakesAttribute`, so every consumer's `using` line
    is unchanged and no consumer re-runs the generator. The doubles reach a
    consumer as an ordinary assembly reference.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="CSharpFunctionalExtensions"/>
    <PackageReference Include="TUnit"/>
    <PackageReference Include="TUnit.Assertions"/>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
